-- wra1-sch1.l4: Welfare Reform Act 2012 (United Kingdom), Schedule 1 (universal credit: supplementary regulation-making powers), part wra1.
-- LAYER 1, the law objectively: it takes nobody's point of view.
-- Source: inputs/ukpga-2012-5.txt, a plain-text rendering made by a converter script, not the official text.
--
-- The raw text prints no paragraph numbers; they are counted by cross-heading, as PLAN.md section 5 does:
--   1 Entitlement of joint claimants, 2 Linking periods, 3 Couples, 4 Calculation of capital and income,
--   5 Responsibility for children etc, 6 Vouchers, 7 Work-related requirements, 8 Good reason.
-- The count agrees with the Act's own references: s 43(3)(l) cites "paragraph 4 of Schedule 1 (calculation of capital and income)"
-- and s 19(6)(b)(ii) is cited from paragraph 5(2).
--
-- A power to make regulations is machinery (inert, reason 2) unless an encoded rule takes its result as an input; then it is
-- encoded as a function that answers from the value the regulations supply and NOTHING when none is supplied.
-- Reading an answer (rule of 2026-10-29, assumed and not ruled by a lawyer):
--   FALSE / JUST FALSE = the text says no in its own words (quoted in a FALSE-kind comment), or a supplied regulation says no
--   NOTHING            = not covered (the reason is in a NOTHING-kind comment)

§ `Schedule 1 paragraph 1 -- Entitlement of joint claimants`

-- "Regulations may provide for circumstances in which joint claimants may be entitled to universal credit without each of them
-- meeting all the basic conditions referred to in section 4." It is an exception power to s 3(2) and s 4 (class E): it can only
-- add an entitlement, so the answer is JUST TRUE when regulations cover the claimants and NOTHING otherwise.
-- src:2632 | Regulations may provide for circumstances in which joint claimants may be entitled to universal credit without each of them meeting all the basic conditions referred to in section 4.
@ref sch 1 para 1 (src:2632-2632)
GIVEN `the claimants are joint claimants` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations provide circumstances that cover these joint claimants` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 1 para 1 joint claimants may be entitled without each meeting all the basic conditions` MEANS
    IF `the claimants are joint claimants`
    THEN
        CONSIDER `regulations provide circumstances that cover these joint claimants`
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the circumstances are for regulations, which are not among the sources
        OTHERWISE NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is for "joint claimants"
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Schedule 1 paragraph 2 -- Linking periods`

-- "Regulations may provide for periods of entitlement to universal credit which are separated by no more than a prescribed
-- number of days to be treated as a single period." The number of days is prescribed, so it is an input.
-- src:2634 | Regulations may provide for periods of entitlement to universal credit which are separated by no more than a prescribed number of days to be treated as a single period.
@ref sch 1 para 2 (src:2634-2634)
GIVEN `the number of days separating the two periods of entitlement` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of days regulations prescribe` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 1 para 2 the two periods are treated as a single period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the number of days regulations prescribe`
    WHEN JUST days THEN
        IF `the number of days separating the two periods of entitlement` AT MOST days
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "separated by no more than a prescribed number of days"; periods further apart are not covered
        ELSE NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the number of days is prescribed
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

§ `Schedule 1 paragraph 4 -- Calculation of capital and income`

-- (5) "In the case of joint claimants the income and capital of the joint claimants includes (subject to sub-paragraph (6))
-- the separate income and capital of each of them." (6) "Regulations may specify circumstances in which capital and income
-- of either of joint claimants is to be disregarded in calculating their joint capital and income." The text says yes; the
-- disregard is an exception a supplied regulation can make.
-- src:2658 | (5) In the case of joint claimants the income and capital of the joint claimants includes (subject to sub-paragraph (6)) the separate income and capital of each of them.
-- src:2659 | (6) Regulations may specify circumstances in which capital and income of either of joint claimants is to be disregarded in calculating their joint capital and income.
@ref sch 1 para 4(5) (src:2658-2659)
GIVEN `the claimants are joint claimants` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations disregard this person's capital and income` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 1 para 4(5) the joint capital and income includes this person's separate capital and income` MEANS
    IF `the claimants are joint claimants`
    THEN
        CONSIDER `regulations disregard this person's capital and income`
        -- FALSE-kind (a supplied regulation says no): sub-paragraph (6) lets regulations disregard the capital and income of either of joint claimants
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- the text's own yes: "includes (subject to sub-paragraph (6)) the separate income and capital of each of them"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the rule is "In the case of joint claimants"
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Schedule 1 paragraph 5 -- Responsibility for children etc`

-- (1) "Regulations may for any purpose of this Part specify circumstances in which a person is or is not responsible for a child
-- or qualifying young person." The Act itself does not say who is responsible; the answer is what the regulations supply.
-- src:2661 | (1) Regulations may for any purpose of this Part specify circumstances in which a person is or is not responsible for a child or qualifying young person.
@ref sch 1 para 5(1) (src:2661-2661)
GIVEN `regulations specify that the person is or is not responsible for the child` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 1 para 5(1) the person is responsible for the child or qualifying young person` MEANS
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the circumstances are for regulations, which are not among the sources; a supplied answer is passed on as it is
    `regulations specify that the person is or is not responsible for the child`

-- (2) "Regulations may for any purpose of this Part make provision about nominations of the responsible carer for a child (see
-- section 19(6)(b)(ii))."
-- src:2662 | (2) Regulations may for any purpose of this Part make provision about nominations of the responsible carer for a child (see section 19(6)(b)(ii)).
@ref sch 1 para 5(2) (src:2662-2662)
GIVEN `regulations make provision under which the couple has jointly nominated the person` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 1 para 5(2) the couple has jointly nominated the person as the responsible carer` MEANS
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): nominations are for regulations, which are not among the sources; a supplied answer is passed on as it is
    `regulations make provision under which the couple has jointly nominated the person`

§ `Schedule 1 paragraph 6 -- Vouchers`

-- (1) "This paragraph applies in relation to an award of universal credit where the calculation of the amount of the award
-- includes, by virtue of any provision of this Part, an amount in respect of particular costs which a claimant may incur."
-- (2) "Regulations may provide for liability to pay all or part of the award to be discharged by means of provision of a voucher."
-- src:2664 | (1) This paragraph applies in relation to an award of universal credit where the calculation of the amount of the award includes, by virtue of any provision of this Part, an amount in respect of particular costs which a claimant may incur.
-- src:2665 | (2) Regulations may provide for liability to pay all or part of the award to be discharged by means of provision of a voucher.
@ref sch 1 para 6(2) (src:2664-2665)
GIVEN `the calculation of the award includes an amount in respect of particular costs which a claimant may incur` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations provide for liability to pay to be discharged by a voucher` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 1 para 6(2) liability to pay all or part of the award may be discharged by a voucher` MEANS
    IF `the calculation of the award includes an amount in respect of particular costs which a claimant may incur`
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): a voucher is available only if regulations provide for one, and they are not among the sources
    THEN `regulations provide for liability to pay to be discharged by a voucher`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): paragraph 6 "applies in relation to an award ... where the calculation ... includes an amount in respect of particular costs"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- (3) "But the amount paid by means of a voucher may not in any case exceed the total of the amounts referred to in
-- sub-paragraph (1) which are included in the calculation of the amount of the award."
-- src:2666 | (3) But the amount paid by means of a voucher may not in any case exceed the total of the amounts referred to in sub-paragraph (1) which are included in the calculation of the amount of the award.
@ref sch 1 para 6(3) (src:2666-2666)
GIVEN `the amount paid by means of the voucher` IS A NUMBER
      `the total of the amounts for particular costs included in the calculation of the award` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`sch 1 para 6(3) the amount paid by means of the voucher is permitted` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may not in any case exceed the total of the amounts referred to in sub-paragraph (1)"
    `the amount paid by means of the voucher` AT MOST `the total of the amounts for particular costs included in the calculation of the award`

-- (4) "For these purposes a voucher is a means other than cash by which a claimant may to any extent meet costs referred to in
-- sub-paragraph (1) of a particular description."
-- src:2667 | (4) For these purposes a voucher is a means other than cash by which a claimant may to any extent meet costs referred to in sub-paragraph (1) of a particular description.
@ref sch 1 para 6(4) (src:2667-2667)
GIVEN `it is a means other than cash` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a claimant may by it to any extent meet costs referred to in sub-paragraph (1) of a particular description` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`sch 1 para 6(4) the means is a voucher` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a voucher is a means other than cash by which a claimant may to any extent meet costs" (a definition)
    `it is a means other than cash`
    AND `a claimant may by it to any extent meet costs referred to in sub-paragraph (1) of a particular description`

-- (5) "A voucher may for these purposes (a) be limited as regards the person or persons who will accept it; (b) be valid only for a limited time."
DECLARE `sch 1 para 6(5) feature of a voucher` IS ONE OF
    `sch 1 para 6(5)(a) limited as regards the person or persons who will accept it`
    `sch 1 para 6(5)(b) valid only for a limited time`
    `sch 1 para 6(5) some other feature`

-- src:2668 | (5) A voucher may for these purposes—
-- src:2669 | (a) be limited as regards the person or persons who will accept it;
-- src:2670 | (b) be valid only for a limited time.
@ref sch 1 para 6(5) (src:2668-2670)
GIVEN feature IS A `sch 1 para 6(5) feature of a voucher`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 1 para 6(5) a voucher may be` MEANS
    CONSIDER feature
    WHEN `sch 1 para 6(5)(a) limited as regards the person or persons who will accept it` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 1 para 6(5)(b) valid only for a limited time` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): (a) and (b) are the two things the sub-paragraph names, and it does not say a voucher may be nothing else
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

§ `Schedule 1 paragraph 7 -- Work-related requirements`

-- "Regulations may provide that a claimant who (a) has a right to reside in the United Kingdom under the EU Treaties, and (b)
-- would otherwise fall within section 19, 20 or 21, is to be treated as not falling within that section." Where regulations
-- do, the claimant is treated as not within the section (class D: a supplied regulation overrides the Act's own sorting).
-- src:2672 | Regulations may provide that a claimant who—
-- src:2673 | (a) has a right to reside in the United Kingdom under the EU Treaties, and
-- src:2674 | (b) would otherwise fall within section 19, 20 or 21,
-- src:2675 | is to be treated as not falling within that section.
@ref sch 1 para 7 (src:2672-2675)
GIVEN `the claimant has a right to reside in the United Kingdom under the EU Treaties` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the claimant would otherwise fall within section 19, 20 or 21` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `regulations so provide` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 1 para 7 the claimant is treated as not falling within section 19, 20 or 21` MEANS
    CONSIDER `regulations so provide`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN `s 3(1) both of these are true` `the claimant has a right to reside in the United Kingdom under the EU Treaties` `the claimant would otherwise fall within section 19, 20 or 21`
    -- FALSE-kind (a supplied regulation says no): regulations were supplied and they make no such provision
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether regulations so provide is not among the sources
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- Applying paragraph 7 to the answer of sections 19 to 21: if the claimant is treated as not within the section, the claimant is
-- not within it; otherwise the section's own answer stands.
@ref sch 1 para 7 (src:2672-2675)
GIVEN `the claimant falls within the section on the Act's own test` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the claimant is treated as not falling within the section` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 1 para 7 the claimant falls within the section once regulations are applied` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the claimant is treated as not falling within the section`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is to be treated as not falling within that section"
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
    -- no regulation applies (supplied and not covering, or not supplied): the section's own answer stands
    OTHERWISE `the claimant falls within the section on the Act's own test`

§ `Schedule 1 paragraph 8 -- Good reason`

-- (a) "Regulations may for any purpose of this Part provide for circumstances in which a person is to be treated as having or
-- not having a good reason for an act or omission." Where supplied, the regulation replaces the plain fact; NOTHING means no
-- such regulation is supplied.
-- src:2677 | Regulations may for any purpose of this Part provide for—
-- src:2678 | (a) circumstances in which a person is to be treated as having or not having a good reason for an act or omission;
@ref sch 1 para 8(a) (src:2677-2678)
GIVEN `the person in fact had a good reason for the act or omission` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations treat the person as having or not having a good reason` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`sch 1 para 8(a) the person is treated as having a good reason for the act or omission` MEANS
    CONSIDER `regulations treat the person as having or not having a good reason`
    WHEN JUST x THEN x
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `the person in fact had a good reason for the act or omission`
